# Week 1 - Day 3

## Overview
This day focused on practicing different types of SQL joins and understanding how tables are connected in relational databases. The exercises covered LEFT JOIN, RIGHT JOIN, FULL OUTER JOIN, and handling unmatched records.

## Objectives
- Practice SQL joins with multiple tables
- Understand relationships between tables
- Learn how to handle NULL and unmatched data
- Improve query writing using real-world scenarios

## Topics Covered

### SQL Joins
- LEFT JOIN
- RIGHT JOIN
- FULL OUTER JOIN
- Joining multiple tables

### NULL Handling
- Identifying missing records
- Handling unmatched rows in joins

### Multi-Table Queries
- Combining students, courses, instructors, and enrollments
- Creating reports using multiple joins

### Data Filtering
- Finding records with no matches
- Filtering using WHERE conditions with joins

## Tools Used
- PostgreSQL
- DB Fiddle
- GitHub

## Work Completed
- Created tables for students, instructors, courses, and enrollments
- Inserted sample data into all tables
- Practiced 10 SQL join queries
- Completed bonus challenge using FULL OUTER JOIN
- Updated schema.sql, queries.sql, and output.txt

## Key Examples Practiced
- Displaying students with enrolled courses
- Finding courses with no enrollments
- Finding students not enrolled in any course
- Displaying instructors and their assigned courses
- Creating reports with student, course, and instructor details

## Challenges Faced
- Understanding FULL OUTER JOIN behavior
- Managing NULL values in joins
- Writing queries with multiple table joins
- Identifying unmatched records correctly

## Key Learnings
- Joins are essential for combining related tables
- LEFT JOIN helps include unmatched records from the left table
- FULL OUTER JOIN displays unmatched rows from both tables
- Proper join conditions are important for accurate results
- Handling NULL values carefully improves query accuracy

## Conclusion
Day 3 improved understanding of SQL joins and table relationships. It also helped in building confidence while working with multi-table queries and handling real-world database scenarios.
